00:15:57,394 --> 00:16:04,497
[SPEAKER_04]: It has always resulted in my harm, harm of others, loss of perspective, and everything else, the swamp of everything else that follows.

194
00:16:05,638 --> 00:16:06,198
[SPEAKER_04]: What do I do now?

195
00:16:06,238 --> 00:16:06,538
[SPEAKER_04]: Nothing.

196
00:16:06,698 --> 00:16:15,482
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, think more about the same swamp, the same context, the same peril, the same... and then you will see, oh, look, mind is now free from that same central desire that was here before.

197
00:16:16,485 --> 00:16:17,605
[SPEAKER_04]: Is the real will present?

198
00:16:19,626 --> 00:16:19,966
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't know.

199
00:16:20,066 --> 00:16:26,008
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, bring up something that used to annoy you, that annoyed you yesterday, that annoyed you ten years ago, that filled you with real will or anger.

200
00:16:27,688 --> 00:16:29,469
[SPEAKER_04]: Have the mind of anger present.

201
00:16:29,569 --> 00:16:31,810
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, so, is this for my benefit?

202
00:16:32,870 --> 00:16:34,050
[SPEAKER_04]: Is this really beneficial?

203
00:16:34,591 --> 00:16:35,971
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, is this really what I want?

204
00:16:36,191 --> 00:16:39,572
[SPEAKER_04]: Even when I'm angry and when I act out of anger, is this the outcome I want?

205
00:16:39,912 --> 00:16:40,092
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

206
00:16:40,952 --> 00:16:43,013
[SPEAKER_04]: I want this to not be here in the first place.

207
00:16:43,713 --> 00:16:45,934
[SPEAKER_04]: So, when does it become a problem?

208
00:16:48,769 --> 00:16:53,031
[SPEAKER_04]: So, I've been making it a problem by trying to get rid of it, or acting out of it, or taking it for granted.

209
00:16:53,671 --> 00:17:00,052
[SPEAKER_04]: Reflect on that new context, and the ill will subside in the face of that which you hate.

210
00:17:00,552 --> 00:17:01,333
[SPEAKER_04]: Let's put it that way.

211
00:17:02,553 --> 00:17:03,693
[SPEAKER_04]: Same then with drowsiness.

212
00:17:03,773 --> 00:17:04,594
[SPEAKER_04]: Is there lack of energy?

213
00:17:04,614 --> 00:17:05,234
[SPEAKER_04]: Is there sleepiness?

214
00:17:05,274 --> 00:17:06,014
[SPEAKER_04]: Is there laziness?

215
00:17:06,074 --> 00:17:08,054
[SPEAKER_04]: Is there unwillingness to make the effort?

216
00:17:08,615 --> 00:17:13,376
[SPEAKER_04]: Then again, reflect, question, verbalize the context in regard to that.

217
00:17:14,380 --> 00:17:16,021
[SPEAKER_04]: So, how much time do I have left?

218
00:17:16,342 --> 00:17:16,742
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know.

219
00:17:17,042 --> 00:17:18,183
[SPEAKER_04]: So, really?

220
00:17:18,303 --> 00:17:21,786
[SPEAKER_04]: Is it going to be the end of the world if I don't go to sleep immediately?

221
00:17:21,987 --> 00:17:23,428
[SPEAKER_04]: Or if I go and do this or do that?

222
00:17:23,568 --> 00:17:28,412
[SPEAKER_04]: Or if I just sit and continue verbalizing these things and thinking about them correctly?

223
00:17:29,153 --> 00:17:31,495
[SPEAKER_04]: And then the energy will come back.

224
00:17:31,515 --> 00:17:32,796
[SPEAKER_04]: And the same goes for worry.

225
00:17:33,718 --> 00:17:34,798
[SPEAKER_04]: Is there anything that would worry me?

226
00:17:34,858 --> 00:17:36,239
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, well, see, that's a problem.

227
00:17:36,699 --> 00:17:41,081
[SPEAKER_04]: If I'm still subjected to worry, it means I'm not dispassionate towards the world, towards the senses.

228
00:17:41,601 --> 00:17:44,402
[SPEAKER_04]: Why am I not dispassionate towards the world, towards the senses?

229
00:17:44,522 --> 00:17:45,863
[SPEAKER_04]: Why am I still subjected to worry?

230
00:17:47,403 --> 00:17:51,225
[SPEAKER_04]: Can it be just, you know, accidentally, not my own doing?

231
00:17:51,705 --> 00:17:55,987
[SPEAKER_04]: Or am I still doing things in regard to the world that are based on a certain degree of

232
00:17:57,013 --> 00:18:11,940
[SPEAKER_04]: Concern, entertainment, distraction, either way, to some extent, I'm still using the world and the content it provides, maybe not the sensual and kind of hateful aspects, but other ways, am I still engaging with the world for the sake of forgetting about myself?

233
00:18:13,381 --> 00:18:14,882
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, well, that's why I'm still subjected to worry.

234
00:18:16,403 --> 00:18:16,943
[SPEAKER_04]: And then doubt.

235
00:18:17,983 --> 00:18:18,824
[SPEAKER_04]: The last one, so...

236
00:18:19,787 --> 00:18:21,107
[SPEAKER_04]: Is the doubt present in his mind?

237
00:18:21,147 --> 00:18:21,307
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

238
00:18:21,347 --> 00:18:21,987
[SPEAKER_04]: Can it?

239
00:18:22,027 --> 00:18:22,528
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, let's see.

240
00:18:23,308 --> 00:18:28,089
[SPEAKER_04]: Do I doubt that all of this practice I've been doing now, in regard to the four hindrances, is that right or wrong?

241
00:18:29,089 --> 00:18:30,569
[SPEAKER_04]: No, I'm pretty clear that it's right.

242
00:18:30,689 --> 00:18:32,290
[SPEAKER_04]: And if anything is to be right, it's closer to...

243
00:18:32,350 --> 00:18:34,730
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, so to this extent, at least, I'm free from doubt.

244
00:18:35,370 --> 00:18:39,211
[SPEAKER_04]: So, to this extent, at least, now, I am free from the five hindrances.

245
00:18:40,111 --> 00:18:41,351
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what is the context of that?

246
00:18:44,172 --> 00:18:45,132
[SPEAKER_04]: There is a state of mind.

247
00:18:45,652 --> 00:18:46,713
[SPEAKER_04]: There is a degree of pressure.

248
00:18:47,193 --> 00:18:48,413
[SPEAKER_04]: There is thinking of pondering.

249
00:18:49,410 --> 00:18:51,311
[SPEAKER_02]: But there is also seclusion from unwholesome states.

250
00:18:52,811 --> 00:18:54,972
[SPEAKER_04]: There is also a certain degree of imperturbability.

251
00:18:56,713 --> 00:19:01,194
[SPEAKER_04]: There is feeling, there is perception, there are obviously intentions.

252
00:19:01,254 --> 00:19:03,555
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, look, I described the factors of the first jhāna.

253
00:19:05,876 --> 00:19:12,558
[SPEAKER_04]: Which, yes, it says, once you have been thoroughly withdrawn from hindrances, the pleasure will be born from that seclusion.

254
00:19:14,119 --> 00:19:16,820
[SPEAKER_04]: But you won't be withdrawn from it magically.

255
00:19:17,547 --> 00:19:19,949
[SPEAKER_04]: You won't be withdrawn from it on account of meditation experience.

256
00:19:19,969 --> 00:19:23,672
[SPEAKER_04]: You'll be withdrawn on account of diligence that we said so many times before.

257
00:19:25,134 --> 00:19:26,975
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's not even a hard work, in a way.

258
00:19:27,175 --> 00:19:27,896
[SPEAKER_04]: Just, okay, yeah.

259
00:19:28,957 --> 00:19:33,240
[SPEAKER_04]: Keep the precepts, don't engage with sensuality, restrain your speech.

260
00:19:34,041 --> 00:19:35,783
[SPEAKER_04]: And when you're secluded, what do I do?

261
00:19:35,843 --> 00:19:36,743
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, well, there you go.

262
00:19:36,904 --> 00:19:37,364
[SPEAKER_04]: What do I do?

263
00:19:37,384 --> 00:19:43,229
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, let me see if the mind is free from sensuality, free from ill will, and rinse and repeat.

264
00:19:49,742 --> 00:19:52,864
[SPEAKER_04]: And then you fall off, and you find yourself... How do you fall off?

265
00:19:53,564 --> 00:19:54,685
[SPEAKER_04]: Can you fall off accidentally?

266
00:19:54,825 --> 00:19:58,547
[SPEAKER_04]: Can you just trip over and, oh, look, I'm back into sensuality and ill-will and so on?

267
00:19:59,708 --> 00:20:00,288
[SPEAKER_04]: Can that happen?

268
00:20:02,669 --> 00:20:03,250
[SPEAKER_03]: Well...

269
00:20:05,211 --> 00:20:11,394
[SPEAKER_03]: I suppose by not becoming... established... You're not established.

270
00:20:12,555 --> 00:20:15,216
[SPEAKER_04]: You couldn't get established, you got distracted, whatever else.

271
00:20:16,577 --> 00:20:17,818
[SPEAKER_04]: That still doesn't mean you fell off.

272
00:20:19,135 --> 00:20:19,856
[SPEAKER_04]: How do you fall off?

273
00:20:20,616 --> 00:20:21,897
[SPEAKER_04]: Can you fall off accidentally?

274
00:20:22,197 --> 00:20:23,058
[SPEAKER_03]: By acting out.

275
00:20:23,358 --> 00:20:23,999
[SPEAKER_04]: By acting out.

276
00:20:24,019 --> 00:20:25,300
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it cannot happen by accident.

277
00:20:25,860 --> 00:20:30,584
[SPEAKER_04]: So, even if you don't make any effort, as long as you don't make the effort to fall off, you won't fall off either.

278
00:20:31,865 --> 00:20:34,347
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the point.

279
00:20:36,829 --> 00:20:39,231
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can forget, but not fall off.

280
00:20:39,251 --> 00:20:39,612
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

281
00:20:39,632 --> 00:20:40,452
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, you can forget.

282
00:20:40,973 --> 00:20:43,335
[SPEAKER_04]: You cannot be diligent enough

283
00:20:44,490 --> 00:20:55,555
[SPEAKER_04]: And, you know, like, you can be careless in that regard, but you're still not falling off for as long as you don't start going back to sensuality, the content, the distraction of the world, and so on.

284
00:20:56,695 --> 00:20:58,496
[SPEAKER_04]: And those things cannot be done accidentally.

285
00:20:59,116 --> 00:21:00,757
[SPEAKER_04]: They're always on the level of choice.

286
00:21:04,139 --> 00:21:09,061
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's the whole point of this, as I said, discerning the extent of thinking and pondering.

287
00:21:10,123 --> 00:21:17,747
[SPEAKER_04]: The extent of that vāci-sankhāra, the verbal thinking, there's no problem with it at all.

288
00:21:19,068 --> 00:21:19,648
[SPEAKER_04]: Quite the opposite.

289
00:21:19,888 --> 00:21:28,753
[SPEAKER_04]: It's actually quite a palpable anchor that you can use to discern and sustain wholesome states of mind, free from hindrances.

290
00:21:29,773 --> 00:21:30,894
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the point of the practice.

291
00:21:30,914 --> 00:21:41,364
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not magically just talking, repeating words to yourself, because that's how you enter samādhi or something, through a mantra or through, as I said before, like, saying what you're doing while you're doing it.

292
00:21:41,424 --> 00:21:41,604
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

293
00:21:43,326 --> 00:21:51,434
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, if you do any of that, it would be for the sake of revealing the state of mind, clarifying it if it's not clear.

294
00:21:53,275 --> 00:21:54,857
[SPEAKER_04]: No, let me stop and think about this.

295
00:21:56,171 --> 00:22:04,174
[SPEAKER_04]: Let me verbalize those thoughts, so I don't get distracted by my own thinking.

296
00:22:08,755 --> 00:22:14,157
[SPEAKER_04]: And yeah, for that, you can't have people talking around you.

297
00:22:16,037 --> 00:22:18,278
[SPEAKER_04]: And the clearer this becomes, then, when you do speak,

298
00:22:20,458 --> 00:22:38,496
[SPEAKER_04]: Engaging in speech, or physical actions, will not... because they've been purified beforehand, as that Madhyamanikāya Sutta says, then when you do engage, it will not be reintroducing that gratuitous overlap that you know it cannot happen.

299
00:22:39,918 --> 00:22:43,161
[SPEAKER_04]: But one thing is to know it, another thing is to actually develop your mind in regard to it.

300
00:22:46,966 --> 00:22:50,228
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's also just brings me back to another point I want to highlight.

301
00:22:50,248 --> 00:22:58,014
[SPEAKER_04]: So, as we said, like, so you can't, you cannot accidentally fall back.

302
00:22:58,034 --> 00:22:58,834
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just impossible.

303
00:23:00,135 --> 00:23:12,264
[SPEAKER_04]: So then, universally speaking, when people do fall back, regardless of the views, regardless of the meditation practices and techniques and what they think meditation is or what meditation should achieve,

304
00:23:14,793 --> 00:23:16,254
[SPEAKER_04]: That is the reason why they fall back.

305
00:23:16,374 --> 00:23:24,581
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not because they haven't done their vipassana retreat long enough, or it's happened because, you know, the mantra was not powerful enough.

306
00:23:24,681 --> 00:23:24,881
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

307
00:23:25,322 --> 00:23:27,784
[SPEAKER_04]: It's because they went and acted towards sensuality.

308
00:23:29,205 --> 00:23:31,707
[SPEAKER_04]: There is no other cause for falling off.

309
00:23:33,148 --> 00:23:39,333
[SPEAKER_04]: Even if you spend rest of your life in completely muddled, ridiculous, mystical views,

310
00:23:40,608 --> 00:23:47,853
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, you might not be getting to the goal of the Buddhist practice, but you're not falling off for as long as you're not going back to sensuality.

311
00:23:48,814 --> 00:23:49,514
[SPEAKER_04]: As simple as that.

312
00:23:50,895 --> 00:24:03,525
[SPEAKER_04]: And when people... And see, people fall off because they chose to act against the precepts, against sense restraint, and while it's right there in the face, the reason for their falling off, they don't see it.

313
00:24:04,265 --> 00:24:07,848
[SPEAKER_04]: They think, oh, it's because I have not been on a long enough retreat.

314
00:24:08,607 --> 00:24:16,149
[SPEAKER_04]: So, next time... and then, next time, they commit even harder, they make even more effort into the area that makes no difference.

315
00:24:17,950 --> 00:24:25,151
[SPEAKER_04]: They go and spend three months in a cell, somebody brings them food, and all they watch is their sensations and so on, and that makes no difference.

316
00:24:25,311 --> 00:24:27,012
[SPEAKER_04]: That was not the reason for their falling off.

317
00:24:28,013 --> 00:24:30,054
[SPEAKER_04]: And it will be not the reason for being picked up.

318
00:24:30,154 --> 00:24:36,118
[SPEAKER_04]: And they might pick up the practice there again, not because of their meditation technique, but because they're withdrawn from sensuality.

319
00:24:36,238 --> 00:24:39,220
[SPEAKER_04]: But they don't see that as the core factor.

320
00:24:40,301 --> 00:24:45,864
[SPEAKER_04]: They think that's all secondary, which is why when they go against it, they also think, oh, that's secondary, it doesn't matter as much.

321
00:24:45,924 --> 00:24:47,265
[SPEAKER_04]: Which is why they go against it.

322
00:24:48,285 --> 00:24:50,247
[SPEAKER_04]: Which is why they take precepts conditionally.

323
00:24:52,988 --> 00:24:57,291
[SPEAKER_04]: And even if somebody is practicing for the sake of deities and whatnot, again,

324
00:24:58,693 --> 00:25:07,739
[SPEAKER_04]: They will fall off from whatever the practice is, or they will not fall off from whatever the practice is, if they act towards sensuality or not.

325
00:25:08,500 --> 00:25:10,261
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, hindrances and so on.

326
00:25:10,981 --> 00:25:12,983
[SPEAKER_04]: But predominantly sensuality, really.

327
00:25:13,903 --> 00:25:18,426
[SPEAKER_04]: Lack of sense restraint and falling back onto gratification of your senses.

328
00:25:19,047 --> 00:25:19,387
[SPEAKER_04]: That's it.

329
00:25:21,889 --> 00:25:23,590
[SPEAKER_04]: That's what Buddha did say in that another sutta.

330
00:25:23,630 --> 00:25:34,694
[SPEAKER_04]: Even if somebody, even if a monk does not develop any distinction at all, remains with his confused mindfulness, no right view, but he keeps the precepts.

331
00:25:34,834 --> 00:25:41,236
[SPEAKER_04]: And even for the rest of his life, he's just in tears and pain of restraint, but he still remains keeping the precepts.

332
00:25:41,616 --> 00:25:44,117
[SPEAKER_04]: He's heading up, not down, so to speak.

333
00:25:49,692 --> 00:25:57,534
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's important to emphasize, see, because you could still... Oh, I've fallen off because I haven't discerned the principle with this this is sufficiently enough.

334
00:25:58,254 --> 00:25:58,414
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

335
00:25:59,014 --> 00:26:05,015
[SPEAKER_04]: You might not have discerned the principle sufficiently enough, so they might not be the results of that discernment, but you haven't fallen off.

336
00:26:05,975 --> 00:26:12,257
[SPEAKER_04]: You've fallen off if, having not discerned the principle with this this is sufficiently enough, then you went and acted towards sensuality.

337
00:26:12,397 --> 00:26:16,017
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why you fell off.

338
00:26:16,117 --> 00:26:19,178
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's that's not something you can just... Oh, how did this happen?

339
00:26:19,970 --> 00:26:35,136
[SPEAKER_04]: Because it's always on the level of conscious choice.

340
00:26:35,676 --> 00:26:42,259
[SPEAKER_00]: I might just like slightly backtrack to what you were talking about before in regards to

341
00:26:43,985 --> 00:26:49,970
[SPEAKER_00]: Actively questioning oneself in regard to the sensual thought that has arisen or could arise, for example.

342
00:26:50,890 --> 00:27:00,257
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you were saying it already, but I thought it might be good to highlight the points that, in this sutta that you've been mentioning a lot, like the two types of thoughts.

343
00:27:03,067 --> 00:27:18,371
[SPEAKER_00]: When it starts by saying, okay, a sensual thought has arisen in me, now, like, I see, okay, this is for my dangers, for my harm of others, harm of both, etc., and then it continues, a non-sensual thought arose in me, or a thought of renunciation.

344
00:27:19,151 --> 00:27:23,832
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of natural, I guess, to think, like, there's a sensual thought,

345
00:27:24,572 --> 00:27:32,915
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, there's a non-sensual thought, that's like a different thing, or it's a different type of object, for example, or I'd have to think...

346
00:27:33,796 --> 00:27:35,416
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not quite that.

347
00:27:35,456 --> 00:27:45,100
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, as in, it's exactly the type of thinking, of seeing the problem or the danger of the sensual thought that has arisen, that constitutes

348
00:27:45,480 --> 00:27:46,861
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the non-sensual thought.

349
00:27:46,881 --> 00:27:47,281
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

350
00:27:47,701 --> 00:27:50,883
[SPEAKER_04]: They're not linearly out there, like you can pick and choose between the two.

351
00:27:50,963 --> 00:27:51,403
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

352
00:27:51,543 --> 00:27:52,924
[SPEAKER_04]: No, it's on the level of the state of mind.

353
00:27:53,204 --> 00:27:59,027
[SPEAKER_04]: That's like, even if for you to discern the sensual thought correctly, first you got to see your mind, whether it's affected by it or not.

354
00:27:59,487 --> 00:28:05,450
[SPEAKER_04]: Because what you think sensual thoughts are, to start with, that's already the result of your sensual thoughts.

355
00:28:06,350 --> 00:28:09,292
[SPEAKER_04]: So, first, physical seclusion,

356
00:28:10,757 --> 00:28:22,806
[SPEAKER_04]: Precepts, physical seclusion, guarding of the sense doors, as we said before, losing or overcoming the concern and joys in regard to the entire world, to any extent, to every extent.

357
00:28:23,827 --> 00:28:31,433
[SPEAKER_04]: So then, your own thinking and pondering the domain, the speech, the creature there, can be outlined, because it's not being interfered with.

358
00:28:32,214 --> 00:28:38,259
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, you get to see what state of mind indues in regard to it, whether it's leaning towards it or leaning away from it.

359
00:28:38,945 --> 00:28:45,006
[SPEAKER_04]: Whether it's... it's inclining towards pleasure or not, whether... and... and that's how you start then cultivating it.

360
00:28:45,667 --> 00:28:49,487
[SPEAKER_04]: First, you have to take that elephant out of that wilderness of its own sensuality.

361
00:28:51,888 --> 00:29:06,151
[SPEAKER_04]: But for as long as the... there is a thread remaining, a possibility for an elephant to still kind of re-engage every now and then, and you don't keep an eye on that, well, to that extent, it's going to remain wild.

362
00:29:06,451 --> 00:29:08,792
[SPEAKER_04]: To that extent, it will not accept

363
00:29:09,576 --> 00:29:12,919
[SPEAKER_04]: You're verbal thinking and directing into the right context.

364
00:29:13,599 --> 00:29:16,161
[SPEAKER_04]: It will only take it, eh... conditionally.

365
00:29:26,389 --> 00:29:27,750
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's also the difference, see.

366
00:29:28,851 --> 00:29:36,537
[SPEAKER_04]: Once the mind is now purified from the hindrances,

367
00:29:37,797 --> 00:29:44,898
[SPEAKER_04]: With the sustained diligence, effort, whenever you're alone, you ponder, you clarify, you let it endure, if it's... oh, it's not central, okay.

368
00:29:45,939 --> 00:29:47,439
[SPEAKER_04]: What is the context of a non-central mind?

369
00:29:47,479 --> 00:29:48,719
[SPEAKER_04]: How does it feel, so to speak?

370
00:29:48,799 --> 00:29:49,839
[SPEAKER_04]: How does it look?

371
00:29:49,859 --> 00:29:52,020
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, I'm now in the state of a non-central mind.

372
00:29:52,080 --> 00:29:53,000
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, let me remember that.

373
00:29:53,380 --> 00:29:55,020
[SPEAKER_04]: Let me pick up the signs and features of that.

374
00:29:55,500 --> 00:30:04,482
[SPEAKER_04]: So, having developed your mind like that, then all those things that you learned before, that you need to learn, such as with this, this is, and the right view, and all of that,

375
00:30:05,358 --> 00:30:06,559
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, it falls into place.

376
00:30:06,679 --> 00:30:13,587
[SPEAKER_04]: Without you needing to force it there, you start recognizing it, because the things that were obscuring it have subsided.

377
00:30:14,908 --> 00:30:19,493
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why, even for the right view in the suttas, the mind is free from hindrances at that time.

378
00:30:21,795 --> 00:30:23,597
[SPEAKER_04]: Usually, thanks to the Buddha in the suttas.

379
00:30:24,157 --> 00:30:26,680
[SPEAKER_04]: But, obviously, sometimes people themselves made the effort.

380
00:30:29,270 --> 00:30:33,392
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's why, like, that's why those things are not seen on the level they need to be seen.

381
00:30:33,752 --> 00:30:36,053
[SPEAKER_04]: Because there are obstructions there.

382
00:30:37,514 --> 00:30:46,638
[SPEAKER_04]: But if, for as long as the person just keeps emphasizing seeing, seeing and seeing it, ignoring what's obstructing, that very seeing they are so keen to do, they won't see it.

383
00:30:47,199 --> 00:30:48,479
[SPEAKER_04]: They won't see it on that right level.

384
00:30:49,600 --> 00:30:51,241
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's the element of faith here.

385
00:30:51,281 --> 00:30:53,742
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, but, but I want, yes, I want to understand it.

386
00:30:54,592 --> 00:31:00,294
[SPEAKER_04]: And, through that wanting of understanding, I have learned that there are things that are obstructing me to understand it.

387
00:31:00,735 --> 00:31:10,338
[SPEAKER_04]: Thus, in order to understand it, let me then, out of faith, put this desire for understanding aside, and actually understand these hindrances first, and free my mind from them.

388
00:31:13,220 --> 00:31:14,100
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, but what if... Oh!

389
00:31:14,360 --> 00:31:15,361
[SPEAKER_04]: Hindrance without.

390
00:31:15,381 --> 00:31:15,621
[SPEAKER_04]: Right.

391
00:31:15,861 --> 00:31:17,401
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, so now I shall understand that.

392
00:31:17,742 --> 00:31:21,183
[SPEAKER_04]: Not by providing answers, but by contemplating,

393
00:31:21,937 --> 00:31:27,199
[SPEAKER_04]: Verbalizing the context in regard to the state of mind affected with doubt.

394
00:31:29,840 --> 00:31:30,860
[SPEAKER_04]: Is this for my benefit?

395
00:31:31,160 --> 00:31:33,961
[SPEAKER_04]: How many times in the past have I been affected by doubt?

396
00:31:33,981 --> 00:31:36,062
[SPEAKER_04]: How many times in the past have I seek three answers?

397
00:31:36,082 --> 00:31:37,903
[SPEAKER_04]: How many times in the past have I found the answers?

398
00:31:39,083 --> 00:31:40,664
[SPEAKER_04]: Did this ever stop?

399
00:31:41,448 --> 00:31:42,048
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

400
00:31:42,088 --> 00:31:42,889
[SPEAKER_04]: Same like sensuality.

401
00:31:42,929 --> 00:31:49,293
[SPEAKER_04]: How many times you seek the gratification, scratch the itch, just to find out now it is even more pressuring.

402
00:31:49,633 --> 00:31:52,515
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, but surely then, this principle of doubt is the same.

403
00:31:54,216 --> 00:32:01,460
[SPEAKER_04]: I engage in satisfying the content of the doubt, providing the answer, so to get rid of the pain of the doubt, means I'm now more under the thumb of the pain.

404
00:32:02,240 --> 00:32:04,962
[SPEAKER_04]: Next time it comes, I'll be even more pressured to act.

405
00:32:05,242 --> 00:32:06,423
[SPEAKER_04]: And is that for my benefit?

406
00:32:06,443 --> 00:32:06,503
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

407
00:32:07,905 --> 00:32:16,052
[SPEAKER_04]: So I shall then, for as long as the mind of doubt is present, also I will have this context of danger, peril, harm, present.

408
00:32:16,693 --> 00:32:20,916
[SPEAKER_04]: So then the mind will turn away from the doubt, from wanting to act out of the doubt.

409
00:32:24,459 --> 00:32:30,164
[SPEAKER_03]: And like you were saying earlier, you can also like test the mind, like what if I never

410
00:32:31,780 --> 00:32:39,085
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, you need to be prepared to never get the answer.

411
00:32:39,105 --> 00:32:40,186
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, that's the point.

412
00:32:40,266 --> 00:32:46,250
[SPEAKER_04]: Because getting the answer, rooted in the intention of getting rid of the pain, means more pain.

413
00:32:46,831 --> 00:32:48,912
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it will never be an answer.

414
00:32:51,142 --> 00:32:54,144
[SPEAKER_03]: You can do that with all the five hindrances.

415
00:32:54,184 --> 00:32:55,405
[SPEAKER_03]: Just test yourself.

416
00:32:56,046 --> 00:32:58,288
[SPEAKER_03]: Like that thing that made me anxious or angry.

417
00:32:58,748 --> 00:32:59,308
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly.

418
00:32:59,368 --> 00:33:03,071
[SPEAKER_03]: Can that make me anxious and angry now?

419
00:33:03,272 --> 00:33:04,853
[SPEAKER_03]: Or can anything else do so?

420
00:33:05,033 --> 00:33:06,454
[SPEAKER_03]: And if it is so, what do I do then?

421
00:33:07,054 --> 00:33:07,695
[SPEAKER_03]: What do I do then?

422
00:33:07,755 --> 00:33:08,516
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh yeah, even more...

423
00:33:08,536 --> 00:33:12,158
[SPEAKER_04]: So, now that his mind is affected with anxiety or fear, what do I do then?

424
00:33:13,079 --> 00:33:13,900
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, there you go.

425
00:33:14,760 --> 00:33:16,442
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the first question I ask myself.

426
00:33:16,482 --> 00:33:17,883
[SPEAKER_04]: Why do I need to do anything?

427
00:33:19,515 --> 00:33:20,616
[SPEAKER_04]: What's the pressure?

428
00:33:20,836 --> 00:33:24,960
[SPEAKER_04]: What's the reason for me wanting to do something about this present state of mind?

429
00:33:24,980 --> 00:33:26,001
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm not acting out of it.

430
00:33:26,321 --> 00:33:27,142
[SPEAKER_04]: I did not create it.

431
00:33:27,162 --> 00:33:28,002
[SPEAKER_04]: I did not invite it.

432
00:33:28,102 --> 00:33:28,743
[SPEAKER_04]: Yet it's here.

433
00:33:29,504 --> 00:33:31,305
[SPEAKER_04]: Why do I need to do anything about it?

434
00:33:32,346 --> 00:33:35,889
[SPEAKER_04]: And if doing anything about it, would that constitute acting out of it?

435
00:33:35,929 --> 00:33:36,770
[SPEAKER_04]: Would it make it worse?

436
00:33:37,911 --> 00:33:38,451
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, yeah, it would.

437
00:33:38,952 --> 00:33:39,932
[SPEAKER_04]: Every single time it did.

438
00:33:40,773 --> 00:33:47,399
[SPEAKER_04]: So then, I shall only remain contemplating the danger in doing something about it, until the mind calms down.

439
00:33:49,067 --> 00:33:54,611
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, if I do something about it, it will be fine, because it won't be rooted in that pressure to do something about it, which is bothering me.

440
00:33:56,713 --> 00:33:58,634
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's why you... Oh, what do I do?

441
00:33:58,714 --> 00:33:59,095
[SPEAKER_04]: What do I do?

442
00:33:59,215 --> 00:33:59,595
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, why?

443
00:33:59,735 --> 00:34:00,676
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, because it's unpleasant.

444
00:34:01,417 --> 00:34:03,898
[SPEAKER_04]: So, I want to do something, because I want to get rid of the pain.

445
00:34:06,140 --> 00:34:07,081
[SPEAKER_04]: Is that for my benefit?

446
00:34:07,982 --> 00:34:09,983
[SPEAKER_04]: How many times in the past I've experienced that?

447
00:34:10,684 --> 00:34:12,425
[SPEAKER_04]: Trying to get rid of the pain through doing something.

448
00:34:13,646 --> 00:34:16,268
[SPEAKER_04]: Accepting pain at the face value and acting out of it.

449
00:34:17,637 --> 00:34:20,398
[SPEAKER_04]: And did that ever remove the liability to pain?

450
00:34:21,498 --> 00:34:27,620
[SPEAKER_04]: Or did it just manage the current pressure and made me feel alright, but then made me more dependent on doing something about it?

451
00:34:30,821 --> 00:34:31,681
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's what you do.

452
00:34:32,641 --> 00:34:36,162
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't act and sustain the context in regard to it for as long as it's present.

453
00:34:38,863 --> 00:34:42,904
[SPEAKER_04]: And the mind will have to turn away, because it never wanted it in the first place.

454
00:34:52,368 --> 00:34:54,749
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, to understand what you're saying.

455
00:34:55,509 --> 00:35:05,974
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you said that this verbalization is... well, it's connected to the first jhāna, basically.

456
00:35:06,794 --> 00:35:07,074
[SPEAKER_01]: But...

457
00:35:08,575 --> 00:35:15,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Should this happen only when you... when you uprooted the hindrances, at least in the present situation?

458
00:35:16,436 --> 00:35:24,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is this engagement with... with the hindrances, like working with them, is already the first jhāna?

459
00:35:25,258 --> 00:35:27,719
[SPEAKER_01]: I presume it's not, because they're still present.

460
00:35:29,039 --> 00:35:33,079
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, I mean, technically, first jhāna, pleasure of first jhāna, it's after.

461
00:35:33,360 --> 00:35:35,800
[SPEAKER_04]: The mind is now thoroughly abiding

462
00:35:36,334 --> 00:35:38,235
[SPEAKER_04]: In that, neither too much nor too little.

463
00:35:39,495 --> 00:35:45,817
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you're not inclining towards the content of the hindrances, nor are you trying to get rid of it.

464
00:35:46,917 --> 00:35:49,798
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you have calmed down within it.

465
00:35:50,958 --> 00:35:58,260
[SPEAKER_04]: But, really, that pleasure of jhāna would not have come, if this engagement in the work has not been completed.

466
00:35:58,700 --> 00:36:01,341
[SPEAKER_04]: And that is, that's why I said in the previous...

467
00:36:01,781 --> 00:36:04,542
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't know which one now, but one of the previous talks, the...

468
00:36:06,180 --> 00:36:14,285
[SPEAKER_04]: The closest to a method for jhāna, it's that, purifying your mind from the hindrances, because that's what... Every sutta that mentions gradual training, that's what it describes.

469
00:36:15,345 --> 00:36:28,293
[SPEAKER_04]: So, keep the precepts, guard the sense doors, moderate in eating, watchfulness of your intentions, and then dwelling in seclusion, and then, first watch of night, second watch of night, third watch of night, and all this diligence after Pīṇḍapāda, before Pīṇḍapāda.

470
00:36:28,633 --> 00:36:32,295
[SPEAKER_04]: What he does is, he purifies his mind from the five hindrances.

471
00:36:33,622 --> 00:36:36,384
[SPEAKER_04]: And just day in day out, that's his work, that's his diligence.

472
00:36:36,564 --> 00:36:46,310
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, after that, his mind is now thoroughly secluded from those five bases.

473
00:36:47,050 --> 00:36:49,272
[SPEAKER_04]: They would keep the mind engaged with unwholesome things.

474
00:36:49,312 --> 00:36:50,833
[SPEAKER_04]: So now it's fully withdrawn from it.

475
00:36:51,313 --> 00:36:54,475
[SPEAKER_04]: And then the pleasure born from that seclusion comes to be.

476
00:36:55,927 --> 00:37:00,330
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's like, yeah, that's the jhāna part, but you realize that pleasure is really just the result.

477
00:37:01,030 --> 00:37:09,056
[SPEAKER_04]: That pleasure of safety is the result of the work of making yourself safe in regard to these five hindrances.

478
00:37:09,136 --> 00:37:13,118
[SPEAKER_04]: Five imperfections of mind, five weaknesses of wisdom, all these descriptions of it.

479
00:37:13,679 --> 00:37:18,882
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the work, that's the method, that's the technique, but good luck doing it mechanically.

480
00:37:21,016 --> 00:37:31,661
[SPEAKER_01]: And the thinking and pondering as a jhāna factor, is it like, is whatever mind thinks about in this state without hindrances?

481
00:37:32,782 --> 00:37:40,125
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, yeah, if you made the effort thoroughly, and that might take a long time to to overcome the five hindrances,

482
00:37:41,210 --> 00:37:50,801
[SPEAKER_04]: To learn how to compose your mind away from them, from these five kind of sticky aspects, they would drag you back into sensuality, or will, and so on.

483
00:37:51,962 --> 00:37:55,266
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, whatever the mind were to think, you won't think those five things.

484
00:37:55,346 --> 00:37:56,727
[SPEAKER_04]: You won't think things within them.

485
00:37:57,328 --> 00:37:59,108
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's what Bodhisattva realized himself.

486
00:37:59,749 --> 00:38:00,149
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, wait.

487
00:38:00,829 --> 00:38:05,830
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, having been withdrawn from the hindrances to maintain in the right context, he recognized, oh, hold on.

488
00:38:06,310 --> 00:38:08,191
[SPEAKER_04]: I haven't had those thoughts anymore.

489
00:38:08,891 --> 00:38:11,692
[SPEAKER_04]: Why don't I just now let this mind roam around?

490
00:38:12,012 --> 00:38:15,773
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't need to keep constantly policing it and sustaining it with the right context.

491
00:38:16,353 --> 00:38:19,814
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's where he started calming down internally, even further.

492
00:38:20,814 --> 00:38:25,355
[SPEAKER_04]: Because he said, first, he was just... the two types of thought, he was just thinking wholesome,

493
00:38:26,650 --> 00:38:31,234
[SPEAKER_04]: Establishing the context of wholesome thoughts in regard to the unwholesome hindrances.

494
00:38:31,995 --> 00:38:35,138
[SPEAKER_04]: And then he said, tireless energy arose in me, day in day out.

495
00:38:36,199 --> 00:38:38,181
[SPEAKER_04]: It was just uninterrupted energy.

496
00:38:39,622 --> 00:38:43,926
[SPEAKER_04]: But that in in and of itself, it's not as nice as a complete peace.

497
00:38:44,326 --> 00:38:45,808
[SPEAKER_04]: It's still nicer than sensuality.

498
00:38:46,573 --> 00:38:50,577
[SPEAKER_04]: Tireless energy, effort, zeal, just effortless.

499
00:38:50,638 --> 00:38:53,761
[SPEAKER_04]: When energy is tireless, it feels effortless.

500
00:38:54,222 --> 00:38:55,243
[SPEAKER_04]: But it's not as peaceful.

501
00:38:55,883 --> 00:38:58,126
[SPEAKER_04]: So then, he recognizes, okay, so why don't I stop this now?

502
00:38:58,166 --> 00:39:01,349
[SPEAKER_04]: Because it was done for the purpose of overcoming the five hindrances.

503
00:39:02,210 --> 00:39:06,415
[SPEAKER_04]: And now, the mind is not even, like, even when I let go of it, it's not going into the hindrances.

504
00:39:06,595 --> 00:39:07,717
[SPEAKER_04]: So, why don't I let go of it?

505
00:39:08,396 --> 00:39:15,360
[SPEAKER_04]: And that was the simile of, like, first, regathering your cow's herd from eating in other people's pastures.

506
00:39:15,901 --> 00:39:21,764
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, these crops that they used to be so inviting are now too distant, too far removed, taken away.

507
00:39:22,344 --> 00:39:24,126
[SPEAKER_04]: So, there is nothing enticing these cows.

508
00:39:24,246 --> 00:39:25,006
[SPEAKER_04]: You can let them roam.

509
00:39:25,046 --> 00:39:27,828
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't need to keep them tied down and hitting them with a stick and so on.

510
00:39:28,348 --> 00:39:30,009
[SPEAKER_04]: Because they're not wandering anywhere.

511
00:39:30,469 --> 00:39:32,971
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, you can even have a little snooze under the tree.

512
00:39:33,471 --> 00:39:34,872
[SPEAKER_04]: You know they will not go anywhere.

513
00:39:35,532 --> 00:39:38,134
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, the confidence is born from that seclusion even further.

514
00:39:39,173 --> 00:39:41,956
[SPEAKER_04]: But you know exactly how it came to be.

515
00:39:41,976 --> 00:39:42,997
[SPEAKER_04]: It wasn't just magically.

516
00:39:43,037 --> 00:39:44,859
[SPEAKER_04]: I just fell asleep and look, cows didn't go.

517
00:39:44,919 --> 00:39:46,001
[SPEAKER_04]: Wow, that's amazing.

518
00:39:46,601 --> 00:39:48,023
[SPEAKER_04]: No, you know why they didn't go.

519
00:39:48,383 --> 00:39:53,349
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you prevented any any reason for their going way beforehand and way thoroughly.

520
00:39:56,613 --> 00:39:58,254
[SPEAKER_04]: So that's the that's the work aspect.

521
00:40:00,754 --> 00:40:19,007
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, then, you're saying that in this dvihā-vitakka, so two kinds of thought, sutta, when he was saying that, well, I see that there is the thought of non-real, non-sociality, in this, at this point, he was already abiding in the first jhāna?

522
00:40:19,627 --> 00:40:24,831
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, now, I mean, the outlining the moment, you know, the point is,

523
00:40:26,524 --> 00:40:41,315
[SPEAKER_04]: If you're withdrawn from the five hindrances, if your mind is on its own, without your input, without your context, not thinking sensuality, not going towards ill-will, not going towards laziness, then that's all you need to be concerned about.

524
00:40:42,035 --> 00:40:43,236
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, focus on that, really.

525
00:40:43,316 --> 00:40:47,800
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, when jhāna starts, from what moment onwards, I wouldn't worry about that.

526
00:40:49,161 --> 00:40:52,303
[SPEAKER_04]: That would be the result of this which needs to be done.

527
00:40:53,288 --> 00:41:02,796
[SPEAKER_04]: But see, if you worry where it starts, or if you still think it's important to know where exactly that pleasure of seclusion starts, means your seclusion will be conditional for that pleasure.

528
00:41:03,897 --> 00:41:11,643
[SPEAKER_04]: But any pleasure that you want, before you were secluded, will be a sensual pleasure, will be underlined by craving.

529
00:41:12,444 --> 00:41:15,246
[SPEAKER_04]: And thus, it will not be the pleasure of seclusion.

530
00:41:16,567 --> 00:41:22,032
[SPEAKER_04]: So you really need to stop emphasizing or even... or just regard it as equally dangerous.

531
00:41:23,933 --> 00:41:25,734
[SPEAKER_04]: Because it would be the pleasure that you want.

532
00:41:26,134 --> 00:41:28,236
[SPEAKER_04]: And the pleasure of jhāna is not something you can want.

533
00:41:30,397 --> 00:41:34,700
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not underlined by lust, underlined by lust, craving, and that's why it's a wholesome pleasure.

534
00:41:36,381 --> 00:41:38,882
[SPEAKER_04]: Because wanting pleasure, it's a hindrance.

535
00:41:38,902 --> 00:41:39,723
[SPEAKER_04]: It's the first hindrance.

536
00:41:40,763 --> 00:41:51,208
[SPEAKER_00]: And people can... it's not unusual in the world for people to restrain themselves, give up lots of things, for the sake of a greater pleasure later on.

537
00:41:51,268 --> 00:42:00,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Or let me, you know, save things, not go to the bar, not go to my usual pleasures, so that I can go on this great holiday.

538
00:42:01,533 --> 00:42:03,094
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, get a reward.

539
00:42:03,114 --> 00:42:04,375
[SPEAKER_04]: Delayed gratification.

540
00:42:04,395 --> 00:42:05,676
[SPEAKER_04]: Delayed gratification, yeah.

541
00:42:05,716 --> 00:42:07,396
[SPEAKER_04]: That's still within the hindrances.

542
00:42:07,456 --> 00:42:09,517
[SPEAKER_04]: That's still meditating with the hindrances.

543
00:42:10,925 --> 00:42:12,166
[SPEAKER_04]: As the Buddha said so many times.

544
00:42:12,386 --> 00:42:19,909
[SPEAKER_00]: So, nobody would say that those that... that way of acting, like restraining myself in such and such ways, so that I can get a holiday later on, is not sensual.

545
00:42:20,029 --> 00:42:21,870
[SPEAKER_00]: It's very obvious that it's sensual.

546
00:42:23,268 --> 00:42:31,455
[SPEAKER_00]: But it is kind of the same principle if somebody does these things for the sake of the pleasure of jhāna.

547
00:42:32,856 --> 00:42:34,338
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's really, that's the criteria.

548
00:42:34,798 --> 00:42:35,458
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the criteria.

549
00:42:35,478 --> 00:42:46,328
[SPEAKER_04]: I think even like the, you know, the later on student commentaries of the suttas, even they kind of figured it out through all the text that, oh yeah, if you want jhāna, you need to stop wanting it.

550
00:42:47,188 --> 00:42:50,972
[SPEAKER_04]: There can be no craving or desire for pleasure present.

551
00:42:52,588 --> 00:42:54,049
[SPEAKER_04]: Because that's still within the hindrance.

552
00:42:54,929 --> 00:42:56,530
[SPEAKER_04]: And because of that, you won't be the jhāna.

553
00:42:58,450 --> 00:42:59,011
[SPEAKER_04]: So, same.

